Research Logbook Entry
Hours Worked: 7 hours
Project: MW–M31 Halo Shape Evolution – Velocity Dispersion Visualization
Date: March 6, 2026


Summary of Work
---------------

Developed a velocity dispersion movie module to visualize the
time evolution of the MW–M31 halo kinematics during the merger.
The goal of this work was to track how random motions of halo
particles evolve as the two systems interact and eventually
coalesce into a single merged halo.

Velocity dispersion was computed from the particle velocity
distribution after centering the system using the halo center
of mass. Particle velocities were shifted into the center-of-mass
frame in order to remove bulk translational motion and isolate
the internal dynamical behavior of the system.

The velocity dispersion was calculated using the standard
statistical definition based on the variance of particle
velocities relative to the mean velocity of the halo.

A movie-generation pipeline was implemented using Matplotlib's
FFMpegWriter framework. For each snapshot, the velocity dispersion
distribution was calculated and rendered as a spatial map of
velocity variance across the halo. Frames were then compiled
into a continuous movie illustrating the evolution of kinematic
structure during the merger.

Key implementation steps included:

• Loading MW and M31 particle data for each snapshot
• Combining particle sets into a single halo system
• Centering particle positions and velocities using the
  center-of-mass frame
• Computing velocity dispersion from particle velocity
  distributions
• Mapping dispersion values onto a spatial grid
• Rendering dispersion maps frame-by-frame using FFMpegWriter

Logarithmic color scaling was applied to improve visualization
of both high-dispersion core regions and lower-dispersion outer
halo regions.

Computational complexity was analyzed and documented in the code.
Velocity dispersion calculations scale linearly with the number
of particles per snapshot, resulting in overall runtime scaling of

    O(N_snapshots × N_particles)

Memory requirements remain modest because only particle arrays
and a spatial dispersion grid are stored at any given time.

Outcome:
A complete velocity dispersion visualization module was created,
allowing the dynamical heating and relaxation of the halo to be
tracked throughout the MW–M31 merger process. This movie provides
a complementary diagnostic to the previously implemented density,
angular momentum, and spin parameter visualizations.
